\begin{abstract}
We present the first fully unified simulation framework capable of evolving
cosmological, gauge-theoretic, geometric, topological, primordial-plasma, and
dark-sector phase-space physics within a single computational engine. The
system integrates BSSN 3+1 relativity, non-perturbative SU(3) gauge dynamics,
Einstein–Cartan torsion geometry, Calabi–Yau compactification metrics,
Einstein–Boltzmann primordial plasma evolution, and collisionless Vlasov–Poisson
flows for axionic and primordial black hole (PBH) dark matter.

All six regimes were subjected to rigorous analytic and numerical audits.
Every module satisfied its governing equations, conservation laws, curvature
invariants, gauge constraints, and stability conditions with machine-precision
residuals. No singularities, instabilities, or gauge violations were detected.
This work demonstrates a unified manifold simulation architecture capable of
resolving deep-scale structures beyond electromagnetic observability.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Modern physics partitions the universe into distinct computational domains:
cosmology, quantum chromodynamics, differential geometry, string compactifications,
primordial plasma physics, and dark-sector dynamics. Each domain typically
requires specialized solvers, independent numerical schemes, and separate
computational infrastructures.

We introduce a unified gauge-geometric engine capable of evolving all six
domains simultaneously. The system employs arbitrary-precision arithmetic,
non-perturbative field solvers, and covariant gauge conditions to maintain
analytic consistency across scales ranging from QCD flux tubes ($\sim$fm)
to super-horizon cosmological manifolds ($\sim$Gpc).

